<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shinran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1173">1173</a>–<a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shinran was a Japanese Buddhist monk, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Hōnen</a> and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a>, or True Pure Land school. Exiled in 1207, he married and lived among ordinary people, teaching that salvation depended entirely on faith in the Buddha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>. His school later became the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a> leagues.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinran.jpg" alt="Shinran" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanbokucho-period artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinran_(Nara_National_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shinran was born on 21 May 1173 at Hino, now part of Fushimi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, to Lord Hino Arinori, of a lesser branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a> that had lost its rank after a scandal. The family was known for scholars and civil servants. His father died early and he was educated by his uncle Hino Munenari. His birth name was Matsuwakamaro, and he later used the names Han'en, Shakkū, Zenshin and finally Shinran. He trained as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monk on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>.</p><p>Dissatisfied with his spiritual life, he went on retreat at the Rokkaku-dō temple in 1201. There he had a vision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara">Avalokiteśvara</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Prince Shōtoku</a>, which directed him to the Pure Land teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Hōnen</a>. He became Hōnen's disciple and gave up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> practices for the recitation of the nembutsu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1207 the retired emperor Go-Toba banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Hōnen</a>'s nembutsu community after two followers were accused of arranging liaisons with court ladies and were executed. Hōnen and seven disciples, Shinran among them, were defrocked and exiled to different provinces. Shinran went to Echigo, and he and Hōnen never met again. He saw the exile as a chance to spread the teaching. He and his wife Eshinni had at least six children there.</p><p>In the 1230s he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kantō region</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, where he lived frugally on gifts from his followers, studying, writing and copying documents. He called himself Gutoku, the Bald Fool, neither monk nor layman. He died at the Zempōin temple in 1263, aged 89, having rejected the usual rites for the dying and held that faith alone ensured birth in the Pure Land. He named no successor and did not mean to found a new lineage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His major work, the Kyōgyōshinshō, is a systematic exposition and defence of Pure Land doctrine. Shinran taught that liberation comes from the entrusting mind, shinjin, awakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>'s compassionate other-power and not by any merit of one's own. The nembutsu is a grateful response to Amida's grace rather than a practice that earns merit. He singled out the Sutra of Immeasurable Life as the main Pure Land sutra.</p><p>He never presented himself as the founder of a sect, but his teaching came to be seen as a school, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a>, Jōdo Shinshū. He has often been compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, a comparison first made in the 16th century by the Jesuit Alessandro Valignano, though the scholar Ducor calls it superficial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shinran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Teachers in later centuries added their own classics, among them the Japanese monks <b>Shinran</b> and Dogen and the Tibetan scholar Tsongkhapa, founder of the Gelug school.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>New Buddhist movements of Honen, <b>Shinran</b>, Nichiren and Zen spread widely.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hino Tanjō-in Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.93,135.82&amp;year=1231" aria-label="Hino Tanjō-in Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7994 900 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shinran"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8444 1170h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8444 1170h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.93,135.82&amp;year=1231">What was here in 1231? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the ignorant exile who sinks into the deeps of the great ocean of human affections, who toils to climb the high mountains of worldly prosperity…&#8221;</p><footer>A lament from his writings (in Buddhist Psalms, introduction) &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Difficult is it for men to find a wise Teacher; so is it also for them to be instructed and to hear the Holy Law. More difficult still is it to receive the True Faith.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 67 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Go forward, O Valiant Souls, seeking the Law though all the worlds fall into flame and ruin, for ye shall have passed beyond birth and death!&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 29 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sin is made one with virtue in its essence, even as ice is one with water. The more there is ice, so much the more water is there.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 156 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When every man is beloved of us, even as the son of our own body, there is the Universal Mind made perfect in us.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 90 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
